<!--
    Arabic translation.

    English, in `values/`, is the source (SPEC §9); this file is a translation
    of it, and it is complete. Translate the contents of the tags, never their
    `name` attribute.

    The register is Modern Standard Arabic — the register Android's own
    Settings are written in — and the vocabulary is arbitrated by the system
    lexicon taken from `framework-res.apk` and `Settings.apk`. Every term and
    the reason it was picked are in `docs/glossary/ar.md`.

    Typography:
      · the script runs right to left; the layouts mirror on `start`/`end`;
      · the comma is ، (U+060C) and the question mark ؟ (U+061F), never their
        Latin shapes;
      · a quotation is set in «…»;
      · a value that opens on a placeholder or on a Latin word carries a
        right-to-left mark in front of it, written `&#x200F;` so that it can be
        seen while reading the file. It is what Android's own Arabic resources
        do: a name or a figure standing at the head of the line would
        otherwise drag the line into logical order, and "Careem BIKE يخدم"
        would come out with the network's name on the wrong side;
      · Arabic writes no apostrophe, so nothing here is escaped;
      · **not a single digit is written in this file.** The application formats
        every figure and Android serves Arabic-Indic digits on an Arabic
        device (SPEC §9); a digit typed by hand would put two numbering
        systems on one line. Where the English spells a number out — "forty
        megabytes" — so does this file.

    Plurals: Arabic distinguishes six categories — `zero`, `one`, `two`,
    `few` (n % 100 = 3–10), `many` (n % 100 = 11–99) and `other` (all the
    rest: 100, 101, 102, 200…). The rules are **modulo a hundred**, so 306
    falls in `few` and 143 in `many`. The numeral is always written
    here, and with a numeral in front of it the noun takes three shapes and
    not six: the plural after `few`, the accusative singular after `many`, and
    the plain singular after `zero`, `one` and `other`. Those three therefore
    read alike, which is Arabic and not an oversight; `two` carries the dual.
    `docs/glossary/ar.md` sets it out in full.
-->
